\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 If you already know 
\f1\b Nash equilibrium, mixed strategies, logit choice models, and competitive pricing
\f0\b0 , the cleanest way to understand 
\f1\b Quantal Response Equilibrium (QRE)
\f0\b0  is:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 QRE replaces the assumption of perfectly optimal best responses with probabilistic responses that become increasingly concentrated on higher-payoff actions as rationality increases.
\f0\b0 \
So instead of asking 
\f2\i \'93Which price is my exact best response?\'94
\f0\i0 , QRE asks:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf0 \'93Given my beliefs about competitors' prices, what probability do I assign to each price, considering that higher-payoff prices are more likely to be chosen?\'94
\f0\i0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 1. Start from ordinary Nash\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 Suppose two petrol stations choose prices pi and pj .\
Your expected profit from choosing pi is\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Ui (pi ,pj )\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 and under Nash, player i chooses\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 pi
\f3 \uc0\u8727 
\f0  
\f3 \uc0\u8712 
\f0 argpi max Ui (pi ,pj
\f3 \uc0\u8727 
\f0  )\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 The crucial assumption is 
\f1\b deterministic best response
\f0\b0 :\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 P(pi
\f3 \uc0\u8727 
\f0  )=1\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 and\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 P(pi 
\f4 \uc0\u57376 
\f0 =pi
\f3 \uc0\u8727 
\f0  )=0.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 This is appropriate if agents are perfectly rational and there are no decision errors.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 2. QRE makes the response probabilistic\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 QRE says that even if one price generates a higher expected profit, other prices can still be selected.\
The canonical 
\f1\b logit QRE
\f0\b0  is\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Pi (p)=\uc0\u8721 p
\f5 \uc0\u8242 
\f0  exp(\uc0\u955 EUi (p
\f5 \uc0\u8242 
\f0 ))exp(\uc0\u955 EUi (p))\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 where:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 EUi (p) = expected payoff from price p\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 \uc0\u955  = 
\f1\b rationality / precision parameter
\f0\b0 \
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Pi (p) = probability of choosing price p\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 This is essentially a 
\f1\b softmax best response
\f0\b0 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 The key parameter is \uc0\u955 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 If\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \uc0\u955 =0\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 then\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Pi (p)=
\f3 \uc0\u8739 
\f0 P
\f3 \uc0\u8739 
\f0 1\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 so every price is equally likely.\
As\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \uc0\u955 
\f5 \uc0\u8594 
\f0 \uc0\u8734 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 the distribution converges toward the ordinary best response:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Pi (p
\f3 \uc0\u8727 
\f0 )
\f5 \uc0\u8594 
\f0 1.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 So you can think of QRE as creating a continuum:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 random\'a0behaviour
\f3 \uc0\u10230 
\f0 boundedly\'a0rational\'a0behaviour
\f3 \uc0\u10230 
\f0 perfect\'a0rationality\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 as \uc0\u955  increases.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 3. The subtle part: equilibrium\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 The interesting thing isn't the softmax itself.\
The interesting thing is that 
\f1\b everyone is responding probabilistically to everyone else's probabilistic behaviour
\f0\b0 .\
For two firms:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \uc0\u963 1 (p)=\u8721 p
\f5 \uc0\u8242 
\f0  exp[\uc0\u955 1 EU1 (p
\f5 \uc0\u8242 
\f0 ,\uc0\u963 2 )]exp[\u955 1 EU1 (p,\u963 2 )]\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 and\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \uc0\u963 2 (p)=\u8721 p
\f5 \uc0\u8242 
\f0  exp[\uc0\u955 2 EU2 (p
\f5 \uc0\u8242 
\f0 ,\uc0\u963 1 )]exp[\u955 2 EU2 (p,\u963 1 )] .\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 A 
\f1\b QRE
\f0\b0  is a fixed point:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \uc0\u963 1 =BR1QRE (\u963 2 ) \u963 2 =BR2QRE (\u963 1 ).\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 That's the conceptual leap.\
In Nash:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 "I know your strategy, so I choose my best response."\
In QRE:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 "I know your 
\f1\b probability distribution over strategies
\f0\b0 , so I choose my own probability distribution according to a noisy/quantally rational response."\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

