\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 12. One particularly important insight\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 QRE isn't simply:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 "Nash equilibrium with randomness."
\f0\b0 \
The deeper idea is:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 The equilibrium object itself is a probability distribution over actions, generated by strategically consistent stochastic responses.
\f0\b0 \
Nash asks:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Which\'a0actions\'a0are\'a0optimal?\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 QRE asks:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 How\'a0does\'a0the\'a0probability\'a0of\'a0choosing\'a0an\'a0action\'a0depend\'a0on\'a0how\'a0optimal\'a0it\'a0is,\'a0when\'a0everyone\'a0behaves\'a0this\'a0way?\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 That makes QRE especially attractive when your empirical object is 
\f1\b a distribution of prices
\f0\b0 , rather than a single equilibrium price.\
And that's probably the most useful way to think about it for a pricing model such as your 
\f1\b ThermoQRE
\f0\b0  work: instead of predicting 
\f2\i "the equilibrium price is \'a31.76"
\f0\i0 , the model can predict something like 
\f2\i "\'a31.76 has a 37% probability, \'a31.75 has 28%, \'a31.77 has 21%,..."
\f0\i0 , with the distribution emerging endogenously from strategic interaction.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\
\
In reality you still have to choose one price...so what's the use of a probability...you them tend to increase your lambda and land on the same result as Nash...debate on this\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Yes \'97 
\f1\b that is a legitimate criticism of QRE
\f0\b0 , and it gets at one of the most important conceptual issues with using QRE for real-world pricing.\
Your argument is essentially:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf0 "The firm doesn't submit a probability distribution to the market. It has to submit one price. So why should I care about a QRE distribution? If I eventually need to select one price, why not just increase \uc0\u955  until QRE collapses onto the Nash best response?"
\f0\i0 \
I think that's a strong argument 
\f1\b if QRE is being used as a prescriptive pricing algorithm
\f0\b0 . But it is much weaker if QRE is being used as a 
\f1\b positive behavioural model
\f0\b0 .\
There are really two different uses of QRE.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
