\f1\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 2. But there's a subtle mistake in saying "therefore QRE is useless"\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 The probability isn't necessarily a prescription for 
\f1\b what you should do
\f0\b0 .\
It can instead describe 
\f1\b how firms actually behave
\f0\b0 .\
That's a completely different question.\
Imagine observing 10,000 daily prices from petrol stations.\
You find:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 When \'a31.74 is estimated to be the profit-maximising price, firms choose it 61% of the time, \'a31.73 18%, \'a31.75 14%, and other prices 7%.\
A Nash model predicts:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 P(1.74)=1.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 That's empirically terrible.\
QRE can say:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 P(p)
\f3 \uc0\u8733 
\f0 e\uc0\u955 EU(p).\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Now the distribution itself is the 
\f1\b object you are trying to explain
\f0\b0 .\
You're no longer saying:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 "The firm should randomise between these prices."\
You're saying:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 "Given strategic incentives, these are the empirically plausible probabilities with which firms select different prices."\
That's a much stronger use of QRE.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 3. Think of it as analogous to discrete-choice demand\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 This is probably the most useful analogy for your pricing background.\
Suppose a consumer has utilities:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 UA =10.0 UB =9.8.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 You don't say:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 "The consumer has to buy one product, therefore the probability model is useless."\
The consumer 
\f1\b does
\f0\b0  buy one product.\
The probability model is useful because you're modelling the 
\f1\b distribution of choices across repeated decisions / heterogeneous consumers / unobserved shocks
\f0\b0 .\
QRE is doing something analogous on the 
\f1\b strategic supply side
\f0\b0 .\
The petrol station ultimately chooses one price.\
But the model says that conditional on its information, incentives, noise, heterogeneity, bounded rationality, etc., the probability of selecting each feasible action is:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 P(pi ).\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 The realised price is one draw from that behavioural process.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 4. But this exposes a deeper question: where does the randomness actually come from?\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 This is where I'd push QRE much harder than the standard textbook presentation.\
If you tell me:\
