\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 So my verdict on your challenge\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 I'd actually 
\f1\b agree with about 70% of your criticism
\f0\b0 .\
The weak argument for QRE is:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 "Real firms aren't perfectly rational, therefore give them probabilities."\
That's not enough.\
And:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 "We have to choose one price, so let's take the highest-probability QRE price."\
is also not a particularly compelling decision theory.\
But the stronger argument is:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 Nash is a theory of strategic equilibrium under exact best responses. QRE is a behavioural model of how observed actions depart from those best responses.
\f0\b0 \
And in pricing, the really valuable thing may not be the probability that 
\f1\b you
\f0\b0  charge \'a31.75.\
It may be the probability that 
\f1\b your competitor
\f0\b0  charges \'a31.74, \'a31.75, \'a31.76, etc.\'97because 
\f1\b your optimal deterministic price can then be calculated against that distribution.
\f0\b0 \
That distinction turns QRE from 
\f2\i "why would I randomise my price?"
\f0\i0  into a much more defensible 
\f1\b stochastic model of the competitive environment
\f0\b0 .\
And there is a genuinely interesting research question lurking here: 
\f1\b whether a QRE-based stochastic competitor model actually produces better out-of-sample pricing predictions than Nash, mixed Nash, or a conventional demand/response model.
\f0\b0  That's where I'd test QRE rather than arguing about it philosophically.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
