\f1\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 8. But there's a critical pricing implication\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 And this comes back to your original objection.\
If I give you:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Q(p)=f(p)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 and you have a good estimate of elasticity, 
\f1\b the demand model is usually doing far more economic work than QRE.
\f0\b0 \
Suppose you're deciding whether to move petrol price from \'a31.70 to \'a31.72.\
The critical question is:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 How many litres do I lose?\
That's elasticity.\
QRE doesn't answer that.\
It answers:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Given the resulting profit consequences and competitor behaviour, how precisely does the firm respond to those incentives?\
So in the hierarchy:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Demand>strategic\'a0interaction>choice\'a0stochasticity\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 for many practical pricing applications.\
Not universally\'97but that's a good default.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 9. This also exposes a potential weakness in using QRE for your ThermoQRE model\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 If you build:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 QRE
\f5 \uc0\u8594 
\f0 price\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 without a serious demand model underneath it, you've essentially built a 
\f1\b game-theoretic pricing toy model
\f0\b0 .\
But if you build:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Demand\'a0forecasting
\f5 \uc0\u8594 
\f0 elasticity
\f5 \uc0\u8594 
\f0 profit\'a0surface
\f5 \uc0\u8594 
\f0 competitor\'a0response
\f5 \uc0\u8594 
\f0 QRE
\f5 \uc0\u8594 
\f0 optimal\'a0price\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 then you're doing something much more interesting.\
And you can actually ask an empirical question:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 Does modelling competitor behaviour as QRE improve pricing decisions beyond a conventional elasticity-based optimisation model?
\f0\b0 \
That's the experiment I'd run.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 10. There's an even deeper possibility\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 You could potentially make 
\f1\b \uc0\u955  state-dependent
\f0\b0 .\
Instead of:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \uc0\u955 =constant,\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 have:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \uc0\u955 t =f(market\'a0volatility,competitor\'a0uncertainty,price\'a0dispersion,time\'a0of\'a0day,information\'a0quality,\'85).\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Then QRE becomes a model where 
\f1\b strategic precision changes with the environment
\f0\b0 .\
For example, firms might behave approximately Nash-like when competitor prices are stable:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \uc0\u955 
\f5 \uc0\u8594 
\f0 high\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 but become much more diffuse when the market is volatile:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \uc0\u955 
\f5 \uc0\u8594 
\f0 low.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Now you're getting something that standard Nash can't represent.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
